      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-07-08">Published July 8, 2026</time></p>

      <div set:html={`
<p>Amazon ended support for 13 older Kindle models in July 2026, cutting off WhisperSync, dictionary lookups, and Kindle Store access on millions of devices. The expected reaction was frustration. The actual reaction — as TechCrunch, PCMag, Android Authority, and How-To Geek all reported this week — is a surge in Kindle jailbreaking.</p> <p>Instead of tossing their perfectly functional E Ink readers, thousands of Kindle owners are jailbreaking them to install <strong>KOReader</strong>, an open-source reading application that supports EPUB, PDF, MOBI, CBZ, DJVU, and nearly every other ebook format in existence. The result: an old Kindle that is actually <em>more</em> capable than a new one.</p> <p>This guide explains what the Kindle jailbreak movement is, how to jailbreak your specific model, how to install KOReader, and — most importantly — how to convert your Amazon Kindle library to EPUB so you can read it on your newly liberated device.</p> <h2>Why Jailbreak a Kindle?</h2> <p>Amazon's 13-model support cutoff affects devices released between 2010 and 2014. The hardware in these Kindles — particularly the E Ink Pearl and Carta screens — is still excellent for reading. E Ink displays do not degrade with age the way phone screens do. A 2012 Kindle Paperwhite still offers the same glare-free reading experience it had on day one.</p> <p>The problem is entirely on the software side. Amazon's firmware is locked down, discontinued from updates, and optimized for the Kindle Store rather than for reader freedom. KOReader solves all of that. It turns a single-purpose Amazon device into a general-purpose ebook reader that answers to you, not to Amazon's servers.</p> <p>The difference is night and day:</p> <table> <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Stock Kindle Firmware</th><th>KOReader on Jailbroken Kindle</th></tr> <tr><td>Native EPUB support</td><td>No (converts via Amazon servers)</td><td>Yes (reads EPUB directly)</td></tr> <tr><td>Format support</td><td>AZW3, MOBI, KFX, PDF</td><td>EPUB, PDF, MOBI, CBZ, DJVU, DOCX, TXT, HTML, FB2, PDB, RTF, CHM, XPS</td></tr> <tr><td>Cloud dependency</td><td>Requires Amazon servers for syncing</td><td>Fully offline, USB or Wi-Fi transfer</td></tr> <tr><td>Font support</td><td>Amazon-selected fonts only</td><td>Any TrueType or OpenType font</td></tr> <tr><td>Reading statistics</td><td>Basic (reading time estimate)</td><td>Detailed (pages per session, estimated time left, reading speed, book completion rate)</td></tr> <tr><td>Night mode</td><td>Inverted (white on black)</td><td>Multiple contrast presets, true dark mode</td></tr> <tr><td>PDF reflow</td><td>Basic</td><td>Advanced (fit to width, reflow text, crop margins, page rotation)</td></tr> <tr><td>OPDS catalog support</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (download books directly from Calibre server, Standard Ebooks, Project Gutenberg)</td></tr> </table> <p>For converter-epub.com readers, the critical feature is <strong>native EPUB support</strong>. Stock Kindle firmware requires you to email EPUB files through Amazon's conversion servers, which strips formatting, changes fonts, and adds Amazon branding. KOReader reads EPUB files directly, preserving your original formatting exactly as the publisher intended.</p> <h2>Which Kindle Models Can Be Jailbroken</h2> <p>The jailbreak landscape varies by model and firmware version. As of July 2026, here is the state of jailbreak support for the affected models:</p> <table> <tr><th>Kindle Model</th><th>Jailbreak Available?</th><th>Method</th></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Touch (4th gen)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Serial port / factory cable method</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Paperwhite (1st gen)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Serial port or software exploit (firmware-dependent)</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle 4 (No Keyboard)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Software exploit via SSH</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle 5 (Basic)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Same as Kindle 4 software method</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle DX / DXG</td><td>Yes</td><td>Serial port method</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Fire HD (1st gen)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Fastboot / custom recovery</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Fire HDX</td><td>Yes</td><td>Fastboot method (requires unlock code)</td></tr> <tr><td>Kindle Paperwhite (2nd gen+)</td><td>Yes (with WinterBreak)</td><td>Software exploit (WinterBreak 1.4+, 2026)</td></tr> </table> <p>If your Kindle is newer than the affected list (7th generation and above), the <strong>WinterBreak</strong> jailbreak tool released in early 2026 covers most models up to the 2024 Kindle Scribe. The older models on Amazon's support-cutoff list are actually <em>easier</em> to jailbreak because their firmware is less locked down than modern Kindles.</p> <h2>Step 1: Jailbreak Your Kindle</h2> <p>The jailbreak process differs by model, but the general approach follows one of two paths:</p> <h3>Software Method (Easier, Kindle 4, 5, Paperwhite 1)</h3> <p>The software method does not require opening the device. You download a jailbreak package, copy it to the Kindle via USB, and trigger the installation through the Kindle's web browser or search bar. The exact steps depend on your firmware version, but the general workflow is:</p> <ol> <li>Check your Kindle model and firmware version (Settings &gt; Device Info)</li> <li>Download the jailbreak package from the Mobileread forums or GitHub repositories (search for your model + &ldquo;jailbreak 2026&rdquo;)</li> <li>Connect your Kindle to your computer via USB</li> <li>Copy the jailbreak files to the Kindle's root directory</li> <li>Safely eject the Kindle</li> <li>On the Kindle, open the browser or search bar and enter a specific command provided in the jailbreak instructions</li> <li>The Kindle will restart with the jailbreak installed</li> </ol> <p>The entire process takes about 15 minutes for most models. The most important precaution: make sure you are using the jailbreak package that matches <em>your exact firmware version</em>. Using the wrong version can brick the device.</p> <h3>Hardware Method (Serial Port, Kindle Touch and DX)</h3> <p>Older models like the Kindle Touch and Kindle DX require a serial port connection. This involves opening the device and connecting a USB-to-serial adapter to the logic board. It sounds intimidating, but it is well-documented and works reliably when the software method is not available.</p> <p>The hardware method is not as difficult as it sounds. The Kindle's serial port pads are clearly labeled on the motherboard. You need a 3.3V USB-to-serial adapter (a common FTDI or CP2102 board, under $10 on Amazon), four jumper wires, and a Torx screwdriver to open the case. Once connected, you access the Kindle's boot console and install the jailbreak manually.</p> <h2>Step 2: Install KOReader</h2> <p>Once your Kindle is jailbroken, installing KOReader takes under five minutes:</p> <ol> <li>Download the latest <strong>KOReader for Kindle</strong> package from <code>github.com/koreader/koreader</code> (look for the release tagged &ldquo;koreader-kindle-&rdquo;)</li> <li>Connect your Kindle via USB</li> <li>Copy the entire KOReader folder to the Kindle's root directory</li> <li>Eject the Kindle and restart it</li> <li>KOReader will appear as a new application you can launch from the Kindle's library view</li> </ol> <p>That is it. No compilation, no dependencies, no configuration file editing. KOReader is a portable application — it runs entirely from the Kindle&rsquo;s storage without modifying the system partition. If you want to uninstall, you simply delete the KOReader folder.</p> <h2>Step 3: Convert Your Kindle Library to EPUB</h2> <p>With KOReader installed, your jailbroken Kindle can read EPUB files natively. The next step is to convert your Amazon Kindle purchases into EPUB format so you can load them onto the device.</p> <p>The process has three sub-steps: download your books from Amazon, remove the DRM, and convert to EPUB. This is the same process detailed in our <a href="/blog/amazon-ending-support-13-kindles-2026/">Amazon ends support for 13 Kindles guide</a>, so we will summarize it here:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Download each book</strong> via Amazon's Download & Transfer via USB feature (Account & Lists &gt; Content & Devices &gt; Books tab)</li> <li><strong>Install Calibre</strong> (<code>calibre-ebook.com</code>) with the DeDRM plugin configured with your Kindle's serial number</li> <li><strong>Import the .azw3 files</strong> into Calibre — the DeDRM plugin strips the DRM automatically during import</li> <li><strong>Convert the books to EPUB</strong> by selecting all books and choosing Convert Books &gt; Convert Individually, setting output format to EPUB</li> <li><strong>Transfer the EPUB files</strong> to your jailbroken Kindle via USB — drag them into the <code>koreader/</code> directory or the main documents folder</li> </ol> <p>Calibre's batch conversion can process hundreds of books in minutes. For more detail on the conversion workflow, see our guide on <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre EPUB library management</a>.</p> <h2>What About DRM-Free Kindle Books?</h2> <p>In July 2026, Amazon began rolling out DRM-free indicators on Kindle product pages and offering EPUB downloads for select titles. This program is still limited to participating publishers and only covers new purchases — not the books you already own.</p> <p>For the 13 affected Kindle models, the DRM-free program offers limited relief. Even if you buy a DRM-free book from Amazon today, the older Kindle's firmware cannot read EPUB directly. You still need KOReader to read it natively on your older device.</p> <p>The combination of DRM-free purchasing plus KOReader is the best long-term strategy: buy DRM-free EPUBs from Amazon when available, sideload them via USB, and read them with KOReader on your fully offline jailbroken Kindle.</p> <h2>Risks and Considerations</h2> <p>Jailbreaking a Kindle is safe when done correctly, but there are real risks to understand:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Warranty void:</strong> Jailbreaking voids any remaining warranty on the device. For the 13 discontinued models, this is irrelevant — they are already past any warranty window.</li> <li><strong>Brick risk:</strong> Using the wrong jailbreak package for your firmware version can brick the device. Always double-check your model and firmware before proceeding.</li> <li><strong>No more Amazon services:</strong> Once jailbroken, the device still runs the stock Kindle software, but Amazon may disable cloud features for jailbroken devices. Since Amazon already disabled these features for the affected models, this is not a new risk.</li> <li><strong>Battery life:</strong> KOReader is actually more power-efficient than the stock Kindle reader for local EPUB files. Expect the same battery life or slightly better.</li> <li><strong>Stability:</strong> KOReader is mature software (first release in 2014) with an active development community. It is more stable than the stock firmware on these older devices.</li> </ul> <h2>Alternative: Skip the Jailbreak, Use Calibre Instead</h2> <p>If jailbreaking sounds like too much effort, there is a simpler path. You can convert your Kindle library to EPUB using Calibre (as described above) and read those EPUB files on a different device entirely — a Kobo, a PocketBook, a Boox, or simply the Kindle app on your phone.</p> <p>The advantage of this approach is that it requires no technical risk and no device modification. The disadvantage is that you lose the excellent E Ink reading experience of your old Kindle hardware. Many readers find that <a href="/blog/color-e-ink-readers-2026/">modern color E Ink readers</a> from Kobo and PocketBook offer a superior reading experience anyway, especially for EPUB files.</p> <h2>The Bigger Picture: Reader Freedom in 2026</h2> <p>The Kindle jailbreak surge of July 2026 is not just about saving old hardware. It is a statement about who controls your reading experience. Amazon's decision to end support for 13 perfectly functional devices sent a clear message: the Kindle is a rental, not a purchase. The jailbreak community responded with a different message: your hardware is yours.</p> <p>KOReader is the key to that freedom. It runs on jailbroken Kindles, on Kobo devices, on PocketBooks, on Android phones, and on Linux computers. It reads EPUB — the open, standards-based ebook format that no single company controls. An EPUB file you convert today will open on KOReader ten years from now, regardless of what Amazon, Rakuten, or any other company decides to do with their platforms.</p> <p>Use <a href="https://converter-epub.com">converter-epub.com</a> to convert individual books between formats, or set up Calibre for managing your entire library. Your books should be yours — and EPUB is how you keep them that way.</p> <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"> <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Is it legal to jailbreak my Kindle?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>In the United States, jailbreaking an e-reader is explicitly protected under the Digital Millennium Copyright Act (DMCA) exemption granted by the Library of Congress for reading devices. Jailbreaking does not violate Amazon's Terms of Service as a criminal matter, though Amazon may refuse warranty service for jailbroken devices.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I still buy books from Amazon on a jailbroken Kindle?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. KOReader runs alongside the stock Kindle firmware. You can switch back to the stock Kindle software to buy and download books from Amazon, then switch to KOReader to read them. The two environments coexist on the same device.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Does KOReader support EPUB with DRM?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>KOReader does not include DRM removal capabilities. You need to remove DRM from your Kindle books using Calibre with the DeDRM plugin before transferring them to KOReader. Once the DRM is removed, the EPUB files work perfectly with KOReader.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Will jailbreaking improve battery life on my old Kindle?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>For local EPUB reading, KOReader is slightly more power-efficient than the stock firmware because it does not make background network calls for WhisperSync, store ads, or cloud services. Many users report 10-20% longer battery life after switching to KOReader for local content.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">Can I sync my reading position across multiple jailbroken devices?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>Yes. KOReader can sync reading progress via a Calibre Content Server, Dropbox, or a self-hosted sync server. You will need to set up a sync target in KOReader's settings, and any device with access to that target will share reading positions and bookmarks.</p> </div> </div> </div> <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"> <h3 itemprop="name">What is the best file format to use with KOReader on a Kindle?</h3> <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"> <div itemprop="text"> <p>EPUB is the best format for KOReader. It supports all of KOReader's advanced features — custom font selection, margin adjustment, line spacing, contrast presets, and reading statistics. PDF is also well supported with advanced reflow options. CBZ and DJVU are supported for comics and scanned books.</p> </div> </div>
      `}/>
